
Dataset: “Toronto Raccoon Activity Index” 
https://open.toronto.ca/dataset/toronto-raccoon-activity-index/
   
 > What software did you use to create your data visualization?

 Python.

 > Who is your intended audience? 

 Torontonians who are interested in the behaviour of local raccoons, and in how their behaviour influences incidents of raccoon-human
 standoffs in the city.
    
 > What information or message are you trying to convey with your visualization? 

 While the data quite logically demonstrates that more confident raccoons are more likely to become engaged in raccoon-human standoffs,
 the number of complaints logged about these standoffs varies quite dramatically, with incidents where no complaints logged, for example, 
 about raccoons logged at a confidence 8 level and who engaged in up to 5 standoffs with humans. This demonstrates the need for further
 complaint logging to ensure that raccoon activity in Toronto is being accurately tracked, particularly given the potential for raccoons
 to carry rabies.
    
 > What aspects of design did you consider when making your visualization? How did you apply them? With what elements of your plots? 

 I wanted my visualization to clearly convey my chosen data features, and to be easy to interpret. After testing out different options,
 including a scatterplot, I determined that the large volume of data was most clearly conveyed via a line graph. I find that the
 different lines on the graph, further differentiated by number of logged complaints, is relatively clear and easy to read (although
 I was not able to fully differentiate the straight line occurring for confident level 10 raccoons, where the data does overlap and
 become trickier to interpret). Overall I think I settled on the clearest possible design given the available data.
    
 > How did you ensure that your data visualizations are reproducible? If the tool you used to make your data visualization is not reproducible, how will this impact your data visualization? 

 My data visualization utilizes publicly available data, and publicly available Python code to create the visualization, so it is reproducible.
    
 > How did you ensure that your data visualization is accessible?

 I initially considered using a colour palette based upon the colours of a raccoon, but the lack of contrast between variations of gray and brown
 would have made the visualization potentially inaccessible, so I instead used a wider range of colours with distinct contrast. I also added the 
 star markers to make the data points more clearly identifiable.
    
 > Who are the individuals and communities who might be impacted by your visualization? 

 Torontonians who may interact with raccoons in their daily lives navigating the city. 
    
